th.CHRONIC DIARRHEA:Chronic diarrhea can result in malabsorption of nutrients, including calcium, which is essential for bone health.NEONATAL HYPOCALCEMIA:Neonatal hypocalcemia is a condition of low calcium levels in newborns, which can affect bone development.RICKETS:Rickets is a childhood disease primarily caused by a deficiency of vitamin D, calcium, or phosphate. This deficiency results in weak or soft bones in children, making them more susceptible to deformities and fractures. The condition highlights the importance of adequate nutrition and vitamin intake during the developmental years.FITATES:Fitates are compounds found in some plant foods that can inhibit calcium absorption, impacting dietary calcium effectiveness.OXALATES:Oxalates are naturally occurring substances in some foods that can reduce calcium absorption, affecting dietary calcium intake.)<|COMPLETE|>FITATOS:FITATOS are compounds found in certain plant foods that play a significant role in influencing mineral absorption, particularly in the context of infant nutrition. These compounds, also known as phytates, are known to inhibit the absorption of calcium, and they are commonly found in cereal bran. Understanding the impact of FITATOS is essential for ensuring adequate mineral intake, especially for infants who are in critical stages of growth and development.ALIMENTOS FORTIFICADOS:ALIMENTOS FORTIFICADOS refers to fortified foods that have been enhanced with additional nutrients to improve their dietary value. These products typically include added nutrients such as calcium and iron, which are essential for preventing deficiencies, particularly in at-risk populations. The primary goal of fortifying these foods is to enhance their nutritional content and support better health outcomes by addressing common nutrient deficiencies. In summary, ALIMENTOS FORTIFICADOS are enriched foods designed to provide essential nutrients and improve overall dietary intake.CALCIO EN LA DIETA:Calcium in the diet is crucial for maintaining bone health and preventing osteoporosis.CITRICOS:Citrus fruits are high in vitamin C, which aids in calcium absorption.K-DOQI:K-Doqi son las gu√≠as que recomiendan un aporte diet√©tico de f√≥sforo para pacientes con enfermedad renal cr√≥nica.ALIMENTOS PROTEICOS:Los alimentos proteicos son fuentes de f√≥sforo que deben ser seleccionadas cuidadosamente en la dieta de pacientes con enfermedad renal cr√≥nica.√ÅCIDO F√çTICO:El √°cido f√≠tico es una forma de f√≥sforo que se encuentra en alimentos vegetales y tiene una biodisponibilidad inferior al f√≥sforo org√°nico.ADITIVOS ALIMENTARIOS:Los aditivos alimentarios son compuestos que pueden contener f√≥sforo inorg√°nico y se utilizan en la industria alimentaria, afectando la absorci√≥n de f√≥sforo en la dieta.BARRIL G.:Barril G. is the author of the phosphorus/protein ratio tables published in 2013 for the Spanish population.PHOSPHORUS/PROTEIN RATIO:The phosphorus/protein ratio is a measurement used to assess dietary phosphorus in relation to protein intake, particularly important for patients with chronic kidney disease.ADDITIVOS ALIMENTARIOS:Additives are substances added to food products to enhance flavor, appearance, or preservation, which can include phosphorus.HYPERFOSFATEMIA:HYPERFOSFATEMIA is a medical condition characterized by elevated levels of phosphorus in the blood. This condition can arise from excessive nutritional intake of phosphate or may be associated with underlying health issues, particularly in patients with renal problems. Management of hyperfosfatemia often requires dietary adjustments to control phosphate levels effectively.QUESO DE BURGOS:Queso de Burgos is a type of cheese known for its low phosphorus content, with a ratio of 13.70 mg/g.QUESOS UNTABLES:Untable cheeses are discouraged due to their high phosphorus content, with a ratio of 48.24 mg/g.LECHE DE SOJA:Soy milk is an alternative to cow's milk with a lower phosphorus ratio, but caution is advised regarding additives.LECHE DE ALMENDRA:Almond milk is another alternative to cow's milk, recommended to be limited for renal patients due to high potassium content.CLARA DE HUEVO AISLADA:Isolated egg whites have a low phosphorus ratio of 1.42 mg/g and are ideal for dialysis patients.CARNE FRESCA:Fresh meat has a phosphorus ratio below 16 mg/g, with recommendations for white meat like chicken and turkey.JAMONES:Ham is recommended due to lower fat content and an acceptable phosphorus ratio between 3.64 and 12.57 mg/g.S√âMOLA DE TRIGO:Wheat semolina is recommended as a cereal option with a lower phosphorus ratio.QUESO BLANCO DESNATADO:Skimmed white cheese is advised due to its lower phosphorus levels compared to other cheeses.CARNE BLANCA:White meat, such as chicken and turkey, is recommended for its lower phosphorus content.CORTES MAGROS:Lean cuts of beef and pork are suggested for their acceptable phosphorus ratios.ALIMENTOS SIN FOSFATOS A√ëADIDOS:Foods labeled "without added phosphates" are becoming available, which is beneficial for phosphorus management.PESCADOS CON RATIO BAJO:Fish with a low phosphorus/protein ratio are recommended for renal patients.RATIO F√ìSFORO/PROTE√çNA:The phosphorus/protein ratio is an important measure for dietary recommendations for renal patients.ALIMENTOS INTEGRAL:Whole foods are generally discouraged due to their high phosphorus content.BAKERY PRODUCTS:Bakery products are discussed in relation to their nutritional content and recommendations for renal patients.COLA BEVERAGES:Cola beverages are mentioned for their high phosphorus content due to additives, making them unsuitable for renal patients.VERDURAS Y HORTALIZAS:"VERDURAS Y HORTALIZAS" refers to a category that encompasses vegetables and greens, which are generally recommended for consumption at least twice a day for a healthy diet. However, for patients with specific dietary needs, the intake of "verdurasy hortalizas" is advised to be limited to 1-2 rations per day. Additionally, it is suggested that certain cooking techniques be employed to reduce the potassium content in these vegetables, ensuring they are suitable for those with dietary restrictions.CARNES O PESCADO:Carnes o pescado are recommended at 1-2 rations per day, depending on the dialysis technique, with specific guidelines for phosphorus/protein ratio.AZ√öCARES SIMPLES:Az√∫cares simples are to be consumed occasionally, individualized according to the dialysis technique.BOLLER√çA Y REPOSTER√çA:Boller√≠a y reposter√≠a are discouraged, especially industrially made products.FESNAD 2010:FESNAD 2010 refers to the dietary reference intake guidelines established for minerals, specifically focusing on magnesium, for the Spanish population. These guidelines provide essential recommendations aimed at ensuring adequate mineral intake among individuals in Spain.ALIMENTOS DE ORIGEN ANIMAL:Animal-derived foods such as dairy, fish, and eggs have higher magnesium bioavailability compared to plant-based foods.ALIMENTOS DE ORIGEN VEGETAL:Plant-based foods generally contain more magnesium but have lower bioavailability due to the presence of phytates and oxalates.ESCAROLA:Escarole is a leafy vegetable that contributes to magnesium intake in the diet.ENDIVIAS:Endives are vegetables that provide magnesium and are included in dietary recommendations.JUD√çAS BLANCAS:Jud√≠as blancas are a type of white bean that serve as a rich source of dietary fiber. These legumes are also recognized for their high magnesium content, making them a nutritious addition to a balanced diet.GARBANZOS:Garbanzos, also known as chickpeas, are legumes that play a significant role in nutrition. They are particularly high in fiber, making them a beneficial addition to a healthy diet. Additionally, garbanzos contribute to magnesium intake and contain 6.7 mg of iron per 100 grams, further enhancing their nutritional value.ANACARDOS:Cashews are nuts that provide magnesium and are included in dietary guidelines.EPIRCE STUDY:A study that reports the prevalence of chronic renal disease in Spain.WILLIAMS TREATISE ON ENDOCRINOLOGY:A comprehensive medical text that discusses hormones and alterations in mineral metabolism.OSTEODYSTROPHY RENAL:A bone disorder that occurs in patients with chronic kidney disease due to imbalances in calcium and phosphorus metabolism.CALCIUM, PHOSPHORUS, AND MAGNESIUM:Minerals that are essential for bone health and are discussed in the context of their metabolism and regulation in chronic kidney disease.DIETARY RESTRICTION:The practice of limiting certain foods to manage phosphorus levels in patients with chronic kidney disease.ADDITIVES:Substances added to processed foods that can increase phosphorus intake, impacting patients with chronic kidney disease.OSTEOPOROSIS SOCIETY OF CANADA:The Osteoporosis Society of Canada provides clinical practice guidelines for the diagnosis and management of osteoporosis in Canada.NUTRITIONAL CLINICAL PRACTICE:Nutritional Clinical Practice publishes reviews and studies related to nutrition and its impact on health.CANADA:Canada is a country that has its own data on Non-Persistent Disease (NPD), reporting a prevalence of 13.3 cases per million in 2012. Additionally, Canada is home to the Osteoporosis Society of Canada, which provides guidelines for the management of osteoporosis, contributing to the country's healthcare framework and support for individuals affected by this condition.JOHNELL O:Johnell O is a co-author of a meta-analysis on smoking and fracture risk published in Osteoporosis International.ODEN A:Oden A is a co-author of a meta-analysis on smoking and fracture risk published in Osteoporosis International.JOHANSSON H:Johansson H is a co-author of a meta-analysis on smoking and fracture risk published in Osteoporosis International.DE LATE C:De Late C is a co-author of a meta-analysis on smoking and fracture risk published in Osteoporosis International.EISMAN JA:Eisman JA is a co-author of a meta-analysis on smoking and fracture risk published in Osteoporosis International.HALLSTR√ñM H:Hallstr√∂m H is a co-author of a study on coffee, tea, and caffeine consumption in relation to osteoporotic fracture risk.WOLK A:Wolk A is a co-author of a study on coffee, tea, and caffeine consumption in relation to osteoporotic fracture risk.GLYNN A:Glynn A is a co-author of a study on coffee, tea, and caffeine consumption in relation to osteoporotic fracture risk.MICHA√ãLSSON K:Micha√´lsson K is a co-author of a study on coffee, tea, and caffeine consumption in relation to osteoporotic fracture risk.BROWN JP:Brown JP is a co-author of the 2002 clinical practice guidelines for the diagnosis and management of osteoporosis in Canada.JOSSE RG:Josse RG is a co-author of the 2002 clinical practice guidelines for the diagnosis and management of osteoporosis in Canada.BRYANT RJ:Bryant RJ is a researcher who has focused on the effects of vitamin E and C supplementation on exercise-induced peroxidation in trained cyclists. In addition to his research on antioxidants and athletic performance, he is also an author who discusses the implications of the new Dietary Reference Intakes (DRI) for calcium, particularly in relation to osteoporosis. Through his work, Bryant RJ contributes valuable insights into both sports nutrition and bone health.BISCHOFF-FERRARI HA:Bischoff-Ferrari HA is an author of a study on the effect of calcium supplementation on fracture risk.REES JR:Rees JR is a co-author of a study on the effect of calcium supplementation on fracture risk.GRAU MV:Grau MV is a co-author of a study on the effect of calcium supplementation on fracture risk.BARRY E:Barry E is a co-author of a study on the effect of calcium supplementation on fracture risk.GUI J:Gui J is a co-author of a study on the effect of calcium supplementation on fracture risk.BARON JA:Baron JA is a co-author of a study on the effect of calcium supplementation on fracture risk.SHIN CS:Shin CS is an author discussing the risks and benefits of calcium supplementation.QUESADA G√ìMEZ JM:Quesada G√≥mez JM is an author discussing nutrition and osteoporosis.URIBARRI J:Uribarri J is an author discussing phosphorus homeostasis in health and chronic kidney disease.NOORI N:Noori N is a co-author discussing dietary phosphorus management in chronic kidney disease.KALANTAR-ZADEH K:Kalantar-Zadeh K is a co-author discussing dietary phosphorus management in chronic kidney disease.GUTEKUNST L:Gutekunst L is a co-author discussing dietary phosphorus management in chronic kidney disease.MEHROTRA R:Mehrotra R is a co-author discussing dietary phosphorus management in chronic kidney disease.KOVESDY CP:Kovesdy CP is a co-author discussing dietary phosphorus management in chronic kidney disease.BROSS R:Bross R is a co-author discussing dietary phosphorus management in chronic kidney disease.SULLIVAN CM:Sullivan CM is a co-author who explores the impact of food additives, particularly phosphorus-containing additives, on hyperphosphatemia in patients with end-stage renal disease. Through their work, Sullivan CM addresses the significant implications these additives have for renal patients, highlighting the importance of understanding dietary influences on their health condition.CARVALHO AB:Carvalho AB is an author discussing the control of hyperphosphatemia in chronic kidney disease.CUPPARI L:Cuppari L is an author discussing the control of hyperphosphatemia in chronic kidney disease.ARNAUDAS L:Arnaudas L is an author associated with Grupo Investigaci√≥n ERC Arag√≥n.GIMENO JA:Gimeno JA is a co-author discussing dietary phosphorus management.MORENO R:Moreno R is a co-author discussing dietary phosphorus management.GARC√çA-MENA M:Garc√≠a-Mena M is a co-author discussing dietary phosphorus management.ALVAREZ R:Alvarez R is a co-author discussing dietary phosphorus management.CUBERES M:Cuberes M is a co-author discussing dietary phosphorus management.SAHDAL√Å L:Sahdal√° L is a co-author discussing dietary phosphorus management.ETAABOUDI S:Etaaboudi S is a co-author discussing dietary phosphorus management.P√âREZ J:P√©rez J is a co-author discussing dietary phosphorus management.NUTRICI√ìN Y ENFERMEDAD:Nutrici√≥n y Enfermedad is a manual discussing nutrition and health, including osteoporosis.MANUAL PR√ÅCTICO DE NUTRICI√ìN Y SALUD:Manual Pr√°ctico de Nutrici√≥n y Salud is a practical guide discussing nutrition and health.REVISTA DI√ÅLISIS Y TRASPLANTE:Revista Di√°lisis y Trasplante publishes research related to dialysis and transplantation, including dietary management.JOURNAL OF RENAL NUTRITION:The Journal of Renal Nutrition publishes research on nutrition in relation to kidney health.NUTRITION IN CHRONIC RENAL FAILURE:Nutrition in chronic renal failure is a topic of discussion regarding dietary management for kidney disease patients.FOOD ADDITIVES:Food additives are discussed in the context of their impact on renal patients and hyperphosphatemia.DIETARY PHOSPHORUS:Dietary phosphorus is a key focus in managing chronic kidney disease and its complications.DIETARY PHOSPHORUS INTAKE:Dietary phosphorus intake is critical for patients with chronic kidney disease and is often monitored.DIEGO √ÅLVAREZ BALLANO:Diego √Ålvarez Ballano is an attending physician in Endocrinology and Nutrition at Hospital Universitario Miguel Servet in Zaragoza, specializing in diet therapy and clinical nutrition.ANEMIA FERROP√âNICA:Anemia ferrop√©nica, also known as iron deficiency anemia, is a medical condition characterized by a deficiency of iron in the body, which leads to a reduction in hemoglobin levels. This deficiency affects the body's ability to transport oxygen effectively, resulting in various clinical manifestations such as fatigue and paleness. It is recognized as the most common nutritional disorder worldwide, primarily caused by a lack of iron that leads to decreased erythrocyte production.

In infants, anemia ferrop√©nica can be associated with the early substitution of breast milk or infant formulas with whole cow's milk before the age of one year. Additionally, this condition can impact the physical performance of athletes due to insufficient iron levels. It may also result from blood loss in the feces, which is often linked to food allergies and gastrointestinal conditions. Overall, anemia ferrop√©nica is a significant health concern that can affect individuals across different age groups and lifestyles.NUTRITIONAL ANEMIAS:Nutritional anemias are anemias caused by inadequate intake of iron, proteins, trace elements, or vitamins.ANGELLES CARBAJAL:Angelles Carbajal is one of the authors of the food composition table referenced in the document, contributing to nutritional science.LUISA CABRERA:Luisa Cabrera is one of the authors of the food composition table referenced in the document, contributing to nutritional science.CARMEN CUADRADO:Carmen Cuadrado is one of the authors of the food composition table referenced in the document, contributing to nutritional science.OLGA MOREIRAS:GASTRECTOM√çA:GASTRECTOM√çA is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of part or all of the stomach. This procedure is often performed for patients suffering from gastric cancer or severe ulcers. Gastrectom√≠a can lead to malabsorption of nutrients due to the significant alteration of the digestive system. It is primarily utilized in the treatment of gastric cancer, highlighting its importance in managing severe gastrointestinal conditions.SANGRADO UTERINO:Uterine bleeding is a common cause of iron deficiency anemia, particularly in women.ULCUS GASTRODUODENAL:Gastro-duodenal ulcers are sores that develop on the lining of the stomach or the upper part of the small intestine, often causing bleeding.HEMORROIDES:Hemorrhoids are swollen veins in the lower rectum and anus that can cause bleeding, contributing to iron deficiency anemia.DIVERTICULOSIS:Diverticulosis is a condition characterized by the formation of small pouches in the colon, which can lead to complications such as bleeding and anemia.ANGIODISPLASIA:Angiodysplasia is a vascular malformation that can cause gastrointestinal bleeding, contributing to iron deficiency anemia.POLIPOSIS:Polyposis refers to the presence of multiple polyps in the gastrointestinal tract, which can lead to bleeding and anemia.ACLORHIDRIA:ACLORHIDRIA is a condition characterized by the absence of hydrochloric acid in gastric secretions. This lack of hydrochloric acid can significantly impact the body's ability to absorb iron, potentially leading to deficiencies and related health issues.PLUMMER-VINSON SYNDROME:Plummer-Vinson syndrome is a rare condition associated with severe iron deficiency anemia, characterized by esophageal webs and difficulty swallowing.HEMOGLOBINA:Hemoglobina is a protein found in red blood cells that plays a crucial role in transporting oxygen throughout the body. It is a key factor in diagnosing anemia, as the levels of hemoglobin are critical indicators of this condition.MUCOSAS:Mucosas refer to the mucous membranes in the body, which can be affected by iron deficiency, leading to symptoms like glossitis and angular cheilitis.GLANDULAS:Glandulas refer to glands in the body that can be impacted by nutritional deficiencies, including those related to iron.SULFATO FERROSO:Sulfato ferroso is a common oral iron supplement used to treat iron deficiency anemia.COLONOSCOPIA:Colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the colon and rectum, often performed to investigate gastrointestinal issues.TOMOGRAF√çA COMPUTARIZADA:Tomograf√≠a Computarizada, commonly known as computed tomography (CT), is a sophisticated medical imaging technique that provides detailed information about the body's internal structures. It is particularly valuable in critical care settings, offering insights into body composition for critically ill patients. This imaging method is instrumental in diagnosing a variety of medical conditions. For instance, it is used to confirm the diagnosis of pancreatitis, assess its severity and complications, and study mesenteric pathology, including the diagnosis of intra-abdominal collections. Additionally, high-resolution computed tomography (HRCT) serves as a specialized diagnostic tool for confirming bronchiectasis and evaluating structural changes in the lungs. Overall, Tomograf√≠a Computarizada is essential for creating comprehensive images of internal organs, aiding in the diagnosis and management of numerous health issues.TROMBOCITOSIS:Trombocitosis is a condition characterized by an elevated platelet count in the blood, which can occur in response to various stimuli, including active bleeding.FIBROCOLONOSCOPIA:Fibrocolonoscopia is a procedure that allows for the examination of the colon using a flexible tube with a camera, often used to diagnose gastrointestinal issues.EMEA:The EMEA, or European Medicines Agency, is an agency responsible for the evaluation and supervision of medicinal products within the European Union. In a significant development, the EMEA approved teduglutida in Europe in the same year that the FDA granted its approval, highlighting the agency's role in ensuring the availability of important medicinal products to the European market.MALABSORCION DE HIERRO:Iron malabsorption is a condition where the body cannot absorb iron effectively, leading to potential anemia.NI√ëOS LACTANTES:Infants under one year old who may require iron supplementation due to dietary needs.HIERRO SACA-ROSA:Iron saccharate is an intravenous iron preparation known for its safety and effectiveness in treating iron deficiency.ENFERMEDAD DE RENDU-OSLER:Rendu-Osler disease is a genetic disorder that can lead to continuous and uncontrollable bleeding, increasing the need for iron treatment.INSUFICIENCIA CARDIACA SEVERA:Severe heart failure is a condition that may require iron supplementation due to increased iron needs.RESECCIONES INTESTINALES:Intestinal resections are surgical procedures that may lead to malabsorption of iron, necessitating iron supplementation.CARNES Y PESCADOS:Meat and fish are dietary sources of iron that should be introduced to infants after six months to prevent iron deficiency.FORMULAS INFANTILES:Infant formulas fortified with iron are recommended for infants who do not receive breast milk.HIERRO CARBOXIMALTOSA:ANEMIA MATERNA:Maternal anemia is a condition characterized by a deficiency of red blood cells or hemoglobin in pregnant women, which can lead to severe health risks for both the mother and the newborn.DEFICIT DE HIERRO:Iron deficiency is a common nutritional issue that can lead to anemia and is particularly prevalent among women in developing countries.TRATAMIENTO CON HIERRO:Iron treatment refers to the administration of iron supplements to individuals with iron deficiency or anemia to restore normal levels of iron in the body.SUPLEMENTOS DE HIERRO:"SUPLEMENTOS DE HIERRO" refers to iron supplements, which are pharmaceutical products designed to increase iron intake, particularly for individuals at risk of deficiency, such as pregnant women. These supplements are commonly used to treat iron deficiency and anemia, helping to restore adequate iron levels in the body.DEFICIENCIA DE HIERRO:La deficiencia de hierro es la alteraci√≥n nutricional m√°s frecuente a nivel mundial, constituyendo m√°s de la mitad de los casos de anemia.GEN HFE:The HFE gene, located on the short arm of chromosome 6, is associated with iron metabolism and plays a crucial role in the regulation of iron levels in the body. Mutations in the HFE gene are linked to hereditary hemochromatosis, a genetic disorder characterized by excessive iron accumulation in the body, which can lead to various health complications.MUTACI√ìN C282Y:MUTACI√ìN C282Y refers to a specific mutation in the HFE gene that is commonly associated with hereditary hemochromatosis. This mutation is the most prevalent cause of type 1 hemochromatosis, which significantly impacts the body's ability to absorb iron. As a genetic alteration, C282Y leads to hereditary hemochromatosis, resulting in an excessive accumulation of iron in the body.MUTACI√ìN H63D:La mutaci√≥n H63D en el gen HFE es una alteraci√≥n gen√©tica que puede asociarse con la sobrecarga f√©rrica, especialmente cuando se presenta en combinaci√≥n con la mutaci√≥n C282Y. Aunque la mutaci√≥n H63D est√° relacionada con la hemocromatosis, su efecto es considerado menos severo en comparaci√≥n con el de la mutaci√≥n C282Y.HIERRO HEMO:El hierro hemo es una forma de hierro que se encuentra en productos de origen animal y es m√°s f√°cilmente absorbido por el cuerpo.HIERRO NO HEMO:El hierro no hemo es una forma de hierro que se encuentra en alimentos de origen vegetal y es menos absorbible que el hierro hemo.√ÅCIDO ASC√ìRBICO:√Åcido Asc√≥rbico, commonly known as ascorbic acid or Vitamin C, is a vital nutrient that plays a significant role in maintaining skin health. This essential vitamin is predominantly found in various fruits, particularly citrus fruits, strawberries, and peppers.HEPCIDINA:Hepcidin is a small peptide that regulates iron absorption and release in the body, playing a crucial role in iron homeostasis.DMT1:DMT1 is a transporter that facilitates the absorption of ferrous iron across the intestinal membrane.FERROPORTINA:Ferroportin is a transporter that exports iron from cells into the bloodstream.HFE:HFE is a gene that plays a crucial role in hereditary hemochromatosis, a genetic condition characterized by excessive iron accumulation in the body. This gene influences iron metabolism and is responsible for encoding a protein that is integral to the regulation of iron levels. Mutations in the HFE gene are linked to certain forms of hereditary hemochromatosis, leading to disruptions in normal iron homeostasis.MACR√ìFAGOS:MACR√ìFAGOS, or macrophages, are immune cells that play a crucial role in the body's defense mechanisms. They are particularly stimulated in the context of inflammatory bowel diseases, where they contribute to the immune response. Additionally, macrophages are involved in iron metabolism, functioning by storing and releasing iron as needed. This dual role highlights their importance not only in immune responses but also in maintaining iron homeostasis within the body.ARTICULACIONES:Joints can also accumulate excess iron, leading to complications in hemochromatosis.S65C:S65C is another mutation related to iron overload when associated with the C282Y mutation in hereditary hemochromatosis.C282Y:C282Y is a specific mutation in the HFE gene that is associated with hereditary hemochromatosis, a condition that can lead to iron overload in the body.BEDCA:BEDCA is a database that provides nutritional information, including data on iron absorption in various foods.AFRICA SUBSAHARIANA:Africa subsahariana is a region where iron overload conditions can occur due to dietary factors.HJV:HJV is a gene associated with juvenile hemochromatosis, affecting iron regulation in the body.SLC40A1:SLC40A1 is a gene that encodes ferroportin, a protein involved in iron transport and metabolism.HETEROCIGOTOS C282Y/H63D:Heterocigotos C282Y/H63D refers to individuals with two different mutations in the HFE gene, which can also lead to iron overload.HH TIPO II:HH tipo II, or juvenile hemochromatosis, is a form of hereditary hemochromatosis that manifests in younger individuals.HH TIPO 2A:HH tipo 2A is a subtype of juvenile hemochromatosis associated with mutations in the hemojuvelin gene.HH TIPO 2B:HH tipo 2B is another subtype of juvenile hemochromatosis linked to mutations in the hepcidin gene.HH TIPO 3:HH tipo 3 refers to hereditary hemochromatosis associated with mutations in the transferrin receptor gene.HH TIPO 4:HH tipo 4 is a form of hereditary hemochromatosis caused by mutations in the ferroportin gene.HH TIPO 5:HH tipo 5 refers to hereditary hemochromatosis associated with mutations in the ferritin gene.HEPATITIS C:Hepatitis C is a viral infection that primarily affects the liver and is known to lead to chronic liver disease. It is particularly significant in patients with hemochromatosis, a condition characterized by iron overload, as Hepatitis C can complicate their health status. The infection poses a risk for individuals with hemochromatosis, highlighting the importance of monitoring liver health in these patients. Overall, Hepatitis C is associated with both liver complications and iron overload, making it a critical concern in the context of liver-related diseases.ESTEATOHEPATITIS NO ALCOH√ìLICA:Esteatohepatitis no alcoh√≥lica is a condition characterized by liver inflammation and fat accumulation, which can be associated with iron overload.HOMOCIGOTOS C282Y:HYPERPIGMENTATION:Hyperpigmentation is a skin condition characterized by dark patches on the skin, often associated with hemochromatosis.FERRIC METABOLISM:Ferric metabolism refers to the body's process of handling iron, which is often disrupted in conditions like hemochromatosis.SOBRECARGA DIET√âTICA DE HIERRO:Sobrecarga diet√©tica de hierro is the condition of iron overload resulting from excessive dietary intake of iron.SOBRECARGA PARENTERAL DE HIERRO:Sobrecarga parenteral de hierro refers to iron overload that occurs due to intravenous iron administration.TRANSFUSIONES DE GL√ìBULOS ROJOS:Transfusiones de gl√≥bulos rojos are blood transfusions that can lead to iron overload in patients receiving multiple transfusions.INYECTABLES DE HIERRO INTRAVENOSO:Inyecciones de hierro intravenoso are intravenous iron injections that can contribute to iron overload.HEMODI√ÅLISIS A LARGO PLAZO:Hemodi√°lisis a largo plazo refers to long-term dialysis treatment that can lead to iron overload due to repeated blood transfusions or iron supplementation.ESTEATOPAT√çA NO ALCOH√ìLICA:Esteatopat√≠a no alcoh√≥lica refers to non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, which can be related to iron overload.HEPATOPAT√çA ALCOH√ìLICA:Hepatopat√≠a alcoh√≥lica is liver disease caused by excessive alcohol consumption, which can interact with iron overload conditions.ANEMIA CON SOBRECARGA F√âRRICA:Anemia con sobrecarga f√©rrica is a type of anemia that occurs in conjunction with iron overload.TALASEMIA MAYOR:Talasemia mayor es una enfermedad hereditaria grave que afecta la producci√≥n de hemoglobina en el organismo. Esta condici√≥n puede requerir tratamientos intensivos, incluyendo trasplantes de c√©lulas hematopoy√©ticas. Adem√°s, los pacientes con talasemia mayor a menudo necesitan recibir transfusiones de sangre frecuentes, lo que puede resultar en una sobrecarga de hierro en el cuerpo.ANEMIA SIDEROBL√ÅSTICA:Anemia siderobl√°stica is a type of anemia characterized by the presence of ringed sideroblasts in the bone marrow, often associated with iron overload.ANEMIA HEMOL√çTICA CR√ìNICA:Anemia hemol√≠tica cr√≥nica is a chronic form of anemia that can lead to iron overload due to increased red blood cell destruction.ANEMIA APL√ÅSICA:Anemia apl√°sica is a condition where the bone marrow fails to produce sufficient blood cells, which can be complicated by iron overload.D√âFICIT DE PIRUVATO KINASA:D√©ficit de piruvato kinasa is a rare genetic disorder that can lead to hemolytic anemia and may be associated with iron overload.ACERULOPLASMINEMIA:Aceruloplasminemia is a rare genetic disorder that affects iron metabolism and can lead to iron overload.ATRASFERRINEMIA CONG√âNITA:Atrasferrinemia cong√©nita is a rare genetic disorder that affects iron transport in the body, leading to iron overload.SOBRECARGA NEONATAL DE HIERRO:Sobrecarga neonatal de hierro refers to iron overload that can occur in newborns, often due to transfusions or maternal iron supplementation.SOBRECARGA ADQUIRIDA DE HIERRO:HORMONA FOLICULOESTIMULANTE:La hormona foliculoestimulante es otra hormona producida por la gl√°ndula pituitaria que es esencial para el desarrollo de los fol√≠culos ov√°ricos en las mujeres y la producci√≥n de esperma en los hombres.ARTROPAT√çA:La artropat√≠a es una enfermedad de las articulaciones que puede ser causada por la acumulaci√≥n de cristales de pirofosfato c√°lcico, resultando en dolor y discapacidad.SOBRECARGA F√âRRICA:La sobrecarga f√©rrica es una condici√≥n en la que hay un exceso de hierro en el cuerpo, lo que puede causar da√±o a √≥rganos y tejidos.DOLOR MUSCULOESQUEL√âTICO:El dolor musculoesquel√©tico puede ser un s√≠ntoma de artropat√≠a y puede mejorar con tratamientos como las flebotom√≠as.FLEBOTOM√çAS:FLEBOTOM√çAS refers to medical procedures that involve the extraction of blood from a patient. These procedures are particularly significant in the treatment of conditions such as hemochromatosis, where they are employed to reduce excess iron levels in the body. By performing phlebotomy, healthcare providers can help alleviate iron overload, which is crucial for preventing serious complications, including cirrhosis and diabetes. Overall, phlebotomy serves as a therapeutic intervention aimed at managing iron levels and improving patient health.ENFERMEDAD HEP√ÅTICA ALCOH√ìLICA:La enfermedad hep√°tica alcoh√≥lica es un da√±o al h√≠gado causado por el consumo excesivo de alcohol, que puede complicar la evaluaci√≥n de la ferritina.HEPATOPAT√çA CR√ìNICA POR VIRUS C:La hepatopat√≠a cr√≥nica por virus C es una infecci√≥n del h√≠gado que puede causar inflamaci√≥n y da√±o hep√°tico, afectando los niveles de ferritina.HEPATOPAT√çA CR√ìNICA POR VIRUS B:La hepatopat√≠a cr√≥nica por virus B es otra infecci√≥n que puede afectar la funci√≥n hep√°tica y los niveles de hierro en el cuerpo.PORFIRIA CUT√ÅNEA TARDA:La porfiria cut√°nea tarda es un trastorno que afecta la piel y puede estar relacionado con la sobrecarga de hierro.IST:IST, or the Iron Saturation Test, is a diagnostic test utilized to evaluate iron levels in the body. It is particularly relevant in the context of conditions such as hereditary hemochromatosis (HH). The test helps in assessing the amount of iron bound to transferrin, which is crucial for diagnosing and managing iron overload disorders.H63D:H63D is another mutation in the HFE gene that, when combined with C282Y, can indicate a predisposition to hereditary hemochromatosis.HH:HH, or hereditary hemochromatosis, is a genetic condition that causes excessive iron absorption and accumulation in the body, leading to various health issues.FAMILIARES DE 1ER GRADO:First-degree relatives of patients with hereditary hemochromatosis are recommended to undergo genetic testing to assess their risk of the condition.FERRITINA E IST:Ferritina and IST are tests used to evaluate iron levels and diagnose conditions related to iron overload, particularly in hereditary hemochromatosis.TRANSAMINASAS:Transaminasas are enzymes that, when elevated, indicate liver damage and are relevant in the diagnosis of hereditary hemochromatosis.CRIBADO POBLACIONAL:Cribado poblacional refers to population screening for hereditary conditions, including hereditary hemochromatosis, to identify at-risk individuals.HOMOCIGOTOS C282TY/C282Y:Individuals with the genetic mutation C282TY/C282Y, at high risk of developing hereditary hemochromatosis (HH) due to a 70% penetrance rate.FERRITINA S√âRICA:Serum ferritin is a blood test that measures the amount of stored iron in the body, important for diagnosing conditions like hereditary hemochromatosis.ELASTOGRAF√çA:Elastography is a non-invasive method introduced in recent years for assessing liver fibrosis, particularly useful in hereditary hemochromatosis.HEREDITARY HEMOCHROMATOSIS:DEFEROXAMINE:Deferoxamine is a medication used as an iron chelator, recommended for patients who cannot tolerate phlebotomy or have contraindications, such as severe heart failure or anemia.DEFERASIROX:Deferasirox is another iron chelator indicated for certain forms of iron overload, such as thalassemia major and chronic hemolytic anemia.HEMATOCHROMATOSIS:Hemochromatosis is a condition characterized by excessive iron accumulation in the body, which can lead to serious health issues, including liver damage and diabetes.HYPOGONADISM:Hypogonadism is a condition that can result from iron overload in hereditary hemochromatosis, affecting hormone production and reproductive health.FLEBOTOMY:ALFA-FETOPROTE√çNA:Alfa-fetoprote√≠na is a protein that is often measured to assess liver health and the risk of hepatocarcinoma in patients with liver conditions.VIRUS DE HEPATITIS B:Hepatitis B virus is a virus that attacks the liver and can cause both acute and chronic diseases, increasing the risk of cirrhosis and liver cancer.VIRUS DE HEPATITIS C:Hepatitis C virus is a virus that primarily affects the liver and can lead to chronic liver disease, cirrhosis, and liver cancer.DIETA CONTROLADA EN HIERRO:A controlled iron diet is a dietary regimen designed to manage iron levels in patients with conditions like hemochromatosis, focusing on reducing iron intake.AMERICAN COLLEGE OF OBSTETRICIANS AND GYNECOLOGISTS:The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists is a professional organization that provides guidelines and practice bulletins related to women's health, including anemia in pregnancy.EUROPEAN ASSOCIATION FOR THE STUDY OF THE LIVER:The European Association for the Study of the Liver is an organization dedicated to advancing the understanding and management of liver diseases. It plays a crucial role in providing clinical practice guidelines for various liver conditions, including Wilson's disease and HFE Hemochromatosis. Through its guidelines, the association aims to improve patient care and promote best practices in the diagnosis and treatment of liver-related disorders.AMERICAN ASSOCIATION FOR THE STUDY OF LIVER DISEASES:The American Association for the Study of Liver Diseases published practice guidelines for the diagnosis and management of hemochromatosis.HEMATOLOGY:Hematology is a field of medicine that deals with blood disorders, including iron deficiency anemia and hemochromatosis.IRON DEFICIENCY ANEMIA:Iron deficiency anemia is a condition characterized by a lack of iron in the body, leading to a decrease in red blood cells.HEMOCHROMATOSIS:Hemochromatosis is a genetic disorder characterized by excessive iron accumulation in the body, often resulting from genetic factors. This condition can lead to various health complications due to the body's inability to regulate iron absorption effectively.PAGOPHAGIA:Pagophagia is a condition characterized by the compulsive consumption of ice or cold substances, often associated with iron deficiency anemia.ANEMIA IN PREGNANCY:Anemia in pregnancy refers to the condition of having a lower than normal number of red blood cells during pregnancy, which can be influenced by iron deficiency.COGNITIVE ACHIEVEMENT:Cognitive achievement refers to the level of intellectual functioning and performance, which can be affected by conditions such as iron deficiency anemia in children.GUIDELINES FOR IRON DEFICIENCY:Guidelines for iron deficiency provide recommendations for diagnosis, management, and prevention of iron deficiency and related anemia.NAT GENET:Nature Genetics is a scientific journal that publishes research in genetics and genomics.FERRITIN:Ferritin is a protein that stores iron in the body and is an important marker for iron levels in the blood.HCC:Hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is a type of liver cancer that can be associated with chronic liver diseases, including hereditary hemochromatosis.GURRIN LC:Gurrin LC is a researcher with a focus on hereditary hemochromatosis and its genetic implications, as well as contributing to studies on food allergies. This dual involvement highlights Gurrin LC's diverse research interests within the fields of genetics and immunology.CONSTANTINE CC:Constantine CC is a researcher who has published work on hereditary hemochromatosis and iron overload.OSBORNE NJ:Osborne NJ is a researcher with a focus on multiple areas of study, including food allergies and hereditary hemochromatosis. Through their work, Osborne NJ has made significant contributions to the understanding of these health conditions, enhancing knowledge in both fields.DELATYCKI MB:Delatycki MB is a researcher focused on hereditary hemochromatosis and its clinical implications.NICOLL AJ:Nicoll AJ is a researcher who has studied iron-overload-related diseases, including hereditary hemochromatosis.WALLACE DF:Wallace DF is a researcher who has investigated the frequency of mutations related to hereditary hemochromatosis.WALTER AP:Walter AP is a researcher involved in studies on hereditary hemochromatosis and iron overload.PIETRANGEL A:Pietrangel A is a researcher who has published work on the pathogenesis and treatment of hereditary hemochromatosis.CLARE M:Clare M is a researcher who has contributed to studies on hereditary hemochromatosis.BOMFORD AB:Bomford AB is a researcher focused on hereditary hemochromatosis and its clinical management.DIXON JL:Dixon JL is a researcher who has focused on the relationship between alcohol consumption and liver disease in the context of hemochromatosis. Additionally, Dixon JL has investigated the connection between iron overload and hereditary hemochromatosis. Through this research, Dixon JL has contributed to a deeper understanding of the implications of these factors on liver health in individuals affected by hemochromatosis.ADAMS PC:Adams PC is a researcher who has published work on screening for hereditary hemochromatosis in diverse populations.ELLERVIK C:Ellervik C is a researcher who has conducted meta-analyses on hemochromatosis genotypes and disease endpoints.BIRGENS H:Birgens H is a researcher involved in studies on hereditary hemochromatosis and its genetic factors.TYBJAERG-HANSEN A:Tybjaerg-Hansen A is a researcher who has studied the genetic aspects of hereditary hemochromatosis.NORDESTGAARD BG:Nordestgaard BG is a researcher focused on the epidemiology of hereditary hemochromatosis.FARGION S:Fargion S is a researcher who has studied prognostic factors for liver cancer in genetic hemochromatosis.FRACANZANI AL:Fracanzani AL is a researcher involved in the study of hereditary hemochromatosis and its complications.PIPERNO A:Piperno A is a researcher who has published work on hereditary hemochromatosis and iron overload.MORRISON ED:Morrison ED is a researcher who has studied the relationship between iron overload and liver fibrosis in hemochromatosis.GANDON Y:Gandon Y is a researcher focused on non-invasive assessment of hepatic iron stores in hemochromatosis.OLIVIE D:Olivie D is a researcher who has contributed to studies on hepatic iron assessment using MRI.GUYADER D:Guyader D is a researcher involved in studies on iron overload and its effects on liver health.AUBE C:Aube C is a researcher who has studied the implications of iron overload in liver diseases.OBERTI F:Oberti F is a researcher focused on the assessment of hepatic iron stores in patients with hemochromatosis.SEBILLE V:Sebille V is a researcher who has contributed to studies on iron overload and liver health.OLYNYK JK:Olynyk JK is a researcher who has studied the relationship between iron exposure and liver fibrosis in hemochromatosis.ST PIERRE TG:St Pierre TG is a researcher focused on the assessment of hepatic iron stores in hemochromatosis.BRITTON RS:Britton RS is a researcher who has studied the effects of iron overload on liver health.BRUNT EM:Brunt EM is a researcher involved in studies on liver pathology related to hemochromatosis.BACON BR:Bacon BR is a researcher who has studied the implications of iron overload in liver diseases.FLETCHER LM:Fletcher LM is a researcher focused on the effects of alcohol on hereditary hemochromatosis.PURDIE DM:Purdie DM is a researcher involved in studies on liver disease related to hereditary hemochromatosis.POWELL LW:Powell LW is a researcher who has studied the effects of alcohol on liver health in hemochromatosis.EASLEY RM:Easley RM is a researcher who has studied reversible cardiomyopathy associated with hemochromatosis.SCHREINER BF:Schreiner BF is a researcher focused on the cardiovascular implications of hemochromatosis.YILL PN:Yill PN is a researcher who has studied the effects of iron overload on heart health.IN√âS LS:In√©s LS is a researcher who has studied arthropathy as a manifestation of genetic hemochromatosis.DA SILVA JA:Da Silva JA is a researcher involved in studies on the musculoskeletal effects of hemochromatosis.MALCATA AB:Malcata AB is a researcher who has studied the clinical manifestations of hereditary hemochromatosis.PORTO AL:Porto AL is a researcher focused on the complications of hereditary hemochromatosis.ALLEN KJ:Allen KJ is a researcher involved in the study of food allergies.DIANA BOJ CARCELLER:Diana Boj Carceller is a specialist physician in Endocrinology and Nutrition at Hospital Universitario Miguel Servet.BEATRIZ LARDIES S√ÅNCHEZ:Beatriz Lardies S√°nchez is a specialist physician in Endocrinology and Nutrition at Hospital Universitario Miguel Servet.ISABEL AZCONA MONREAL:Isabel Azcona Monreal is a Resident Intern in Endocrinology and Nutrition at Hospital Universitario Miguel Servet.MUTACI√ìN DEL GEN ATP7A:The entity "MUTACI√ìN DEL GEN ATP7A" refers to a genetic alteration that can lead to the disease known as Menkes disease, as well as other disorders related to copper metabolism. This mutation specifically impacts the function of the ATP7A gene, which is crucial for the regulation and transport of copper within the body.MUTACI√ìN DEL GEN ATP7B:Mutaci√≥n del gen ATP7B is a genetic mutation associated with the Enfermedad de Wilson.FLETCHER, LM:Fletcher, LM is an author of a study discussing the effects of excess alcohol on cirrhosis prevalence in hereditary hemochromatosis.DIXON, JL:Dixon, JL is a co-author of a study on the effects of excess alcohol on cirrhosis in hereditary hemochromatosis.PURDIE, DM:Purdie, DM is a co-author of a study discussing the relationship between excess alcohol and cirrhosis in hereditary hemochromatosis.POWELL, LW:Powell, LW is a co-author of a study on the effects of excess alcohol on cirrhosis in hereditary hemochromatosis.ROUGHEAD, ZK:Roughead, ZK is an author of a study on the inhibitory effects of dietary calcium on iron uptake in humans.ZITO, CA:Zito, CA is a co-author of a study on the effects of dietary calcium on iron absorption.HUNT, JR:Hunt, JR is a co-author of a study discussing the effects of dietary calcium on iron uptake.KALTWASSER, JP:Kaltwasser, JP is an author of a clinical trial on the effects of tea drinking on iron accumulation in genetic hemochromatosis.WERNER, E:Werner, E is a co-author of a clinical trial discussing the effects of tea drinking on iron accumulation.SCHALK, K:Schalk, K is a co-author of a clinical trial on the effects of tea drinking on iron accumulation.HANSEN, C:Hansen, C is a co-author of a clinical trial discussing the effects of tea drinking on iron accumulation.GOTTSCHALK, R:Gottschalk, R is a co-author of a clinical trial on the effects of tea drinking on iron accumulation.SEIDL, C:Seidl, C is a co-author of a clinical trial discussing the effects of tea drinking on iron accumulation.BROWN, KE:Brown, KE is an author of a study on the effect of vitamin E supplementation on hepatic fibrogenesis in chronic dietary iron overload.POULOS, JE:Poulos, JE is a co-author of a study discussing the effects of vitamin E on hepatic fibrogenesis.SOWEID, AM:Soweid, AM is a co-author of a study on the effects of vitamin E supplementation on hepatic fibrogenesis.RAMM, GA:Ramm, GA is a co-author of a study discussing the effects of vitamin E on hepatic fibrogenesis.O'NEILL, R:O'Neill, R is a co-author of a study on the effects of vitamin E supplementation on hepatic fibrogenesis.BRITTON, R:Britton, R is a co-author of a study discussing the effects of vitamin E on hepatic fibrogenesis.SHINODA, S:Shinoda, S is an author of a study on pathogenic factors of vibrios, particularly Vibrio vulnificus.VIBRIO VULNIFICUS:Vibrio vulnificus is a pathogenic bacterium associated with various infections, particularly in individuals with liver disease.CUPROENZIMAS:CUPROENZIMAS refers to enzymes that contain copper and are essential for various biochemical processes in the body. These processes include electron transport and neurotransmitter metabolism, highlighting the critical role that copper-containing enzymes play in maintaining physiological functions.M√öSCULOS:M√öSCULOS are tissues in the body that play a crucial role in physical activity, requiring energy primarily in the form of glycogen and glucose to function effectively. Additionally, these muscles depend on copper for various metabolic functions, highlighting the importance of both energy sources and essential minerals in maintaining muscle health and performance.TEJIDO EL√ÅSTICO:Elastic tissue is a type of connective tissue that requires copper for its synthesis and maintenance.NOREPINEFRINA:Norepinephrine is a neurotransmitter that is derived from dopamine and plays a role in the body's response to stress.MELANINA:Melanin is a pigment responsible for the color of skin, hair, and eyes, and its synthesis is influenced by copper-containing enzymes.TIROSINASA:Tyrosinase is an enzyme that catalyzes the conversion of tyrosine to melanin and is dependent on copper for its activity.SUPER√ìXIDO DISMUTASA:Super√≥xido dismutasa is a copper-containing enzyme that plays a crucial role in protecting cells from oxidative damage. It catalyzes the conversion of superoxide radicals, which are harmful byproducts of cellular metabolism, into oxygen and hydrogen peroxide. By facilitating this conversion, super√≥xido dismutasa helps to mitigate the effects of oxidative stress, thereby safeguarding cellular integrity and function.NEUROTRANSMISORES:Neurotransmitters are chemical messengers in the nervous system that are influenced by copper-containing enzymes in their metabolism.CERULOPLASMINA:Ceruloplasmina, also known as ceruloplasmin, is a glycoprotein that plays a vital role in copper metabolism and transport in the body. It is a protein that carries copper in the blood, containing seven copper atoms per molecule. Ceruloplasmina is significant in diagnosing copper metabolism disorders, particularly Wilson's disease, as its levels are often measured to assess copper deposits in the body. Additionally, it is involved in iron metabolism, functioning as ferroxidase I. Overall, ceruloplasmina serves as an important indicator of copper levels and is crucial for maintaining proper copper and iron homeostasis in the organism.ATP7A:ATP7A is a gene that encodes a copper-transporting ATPase, which is essential for copper metabolism and its incorporation into proteins. This gene is associated with several disorders, including a condition resembling Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease, which leads to neuropathy in adults. Additionally, ATP7A is linked to Menkes disease and Wilson's disease, both of which also affect copper metabolism. Overall, ATP7A plays a critical role in maintaining proper copper levels in the body, and its dysfunction can result in significant neurological and metabolic issues.ATP7B:ATP7B is a gene located on chromosome 13 that plays a critical role in copper transport and the elimination of excess copper from the body. It is associated with Wilson's disease, a genetic disorder characterized by the accumulation of copper in various tissues due to mutations in the ATP7B gene. The analysis of ATP7B is essential for the genetic diagnosis of Wilson's disease and for conducting family screening to identify at-risk individuals.ENFERMEDAD DE MENKES:Menkes disease is a genetic disorder caused by mutations in the ATP7A gene, which results in a deficiency of copper in the body.DIAMINOOXIDASA:Diamine oxidase is a copper-containing enzyme that inactivates histamine in the body, affecting digestive processes and allergic reactions.HISTAMINA:Histamina is a chemical compound that plays a crucial role in local immune responses and the regulation of various physiological functions, particularly in the gut. It is classified as a vasoactive amine, which means it can affect blood vessel dilation and permeability. Histamine is also associated with food intolerance, as it can be found in a wide range of foods and may trigger adverse reactions in sensitive individuals. In the context of allergic reactions, histamine is released and can lead to symptoms such as urticaria (hives) and headaches. Overall, histamine is significant not only for its physiological functions but also for its involvement in allergic responses and food intolerances.METALOTIONEINAS:Metallothioneins are proteins that bind heavy metals, including copper, and play a role in metal metabolism and detoxification.REACCIONES AL√âRGICAS:"REACCIONES AL√âRGICAS" refer to immune responses that occur when the body reacts to substances it perceives as harmful, known as allergens. These reactions often involve the release of histamine and can vary in severity and type of symptoms. The immune system's response to these allergens can manifest in various ways, highlighting the diverse nature of allergic reactions.BALANCE DE COBRE:El balance de cobre en el organismo se refiere a la cantidad de cobre que se ingiere, se absorbe y se elimina, siendo fundamental para la salud.NIVELES PLASM√ÅTICOS DE COBRE:Los niveles plasm√°ticos de cobre son medidos para evaluar la cantidad de cobre en el organismo y su relaci√≥n con diversas condiciones de salud.ALIMENTOS RICOS EN COBRE:Los alimentos ricos en cobre, como las ostras y v√≠sceras animales, son importantes para cubrir las necesidades diarias de este mineral.AGUA POTABLE:El agua potable puede contener cobre y contribuir a la ingesta diaria de este mineral, aunque en cantidades variables.CANTIDAD DE COBRE:S√çNDROME DEL CUERNO OCCIPITAL:Trastorno caracterizado por la presencia de "cuernos occipitales" y calcificaciones en la uni√≥n del cr√°neo.CUERPO HUMANO:El organismo que puede verse afectado por la acumulaci√≥n de cobre debido a trastornos gen√©ticos.CUPROPLASMINA:Prote√≠na que transporta cobre en la sangre, cuya deficiencia puede estar relacionada con la enfermedad de Wilson.ESPR√öE:Trastorno intestinal que puede causar deficiencia de cobre.ENFERMEDADES DEL COL√ÅGENO:Grupo de trastornos que pueden estar relacionados con niveles elevados de cobre en el cuerpo.ANEMIAS HIPOCR√ìMICAS:Condici√≥n que puede estar asociada con niveles bajos de cobre.ASMA BRONQUIAL:Enfermedad respiratoria que puede estar relacionada con niveles elevados de cobre.HEMOPAT√çAS MALIGNAS:Grupo de enfermedades que pueden estar asociadas con niveles elevados de cobre.LUPUS:Enfermedad autoinmune que puede estar relacionada con niveles elevados de cobre.FIEBRE REUM√ÅTICA:Enfermedad inflamatoria que puede estar asociada con niveles elevados de cobre.INFECCIONES GRAVES:Condiciones que pueden causar niveles elevados de cobre en el cuerpo.INTOXICACIONES:Condici√≥n que puede llevar a niveles elevados de cobre en el organismo.ALIMENTOS DIET√âTICOS:Alimentos regulados en Espa√±a que deben aportar un m√≠nimo de cobre para usos m√©dicos especiales.C√ìRNEA:The cornea is affected in Wilson's disease due to copper deposits, which can lead to vision problems.RI√ëONES:Ri√±ones, or kidneys, are organ meats known for their high levels of purines, which can contribute to the risk of hyperuricemia. Additionally, the kidneys are affected by Wilson's disease, a condition characterized by the accumulation of copper in the body, leading to potential renal dysfunction.ESTERNOCLEIDOMASTOIDEO:The sternocleidomastoid muscle is part of the muscular system and may be referenced in relation to conditions affecting copper metabolism.HERNIAS INGUINALES:Inguinal hernias are a condition that may occur in individuals with certain genetic disorders, including those affecting copper metabolism.DIVERT√çCULOS DE LA VEJIGA:Bladder diverticula are a condition that may be associated with genetic disorders affecting copper metabolism.TORTUOSIDAD VASCULAR:Vascular tortuosity is a condition that may be observed in patients with disorders related to copper metabolism.NEUROPAT√çA DISTAL:Distal neuropathy is a condition that can occur in adults with disorders like Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease, which may be related to copper metabolism.DEFORMIDADES DE PIE CAVUS:Cavus foot deformities can occur in individuals with neuropathy related to copper metabolism disorders.NIVELES DE COBRE:Copper levels are critical in diagnosing and managing disorders related to copper metabolism.AASLD:The American Association for the Study of Liver Diseases (AASLD), known in Spanish as la Asociaci√≥n Americana para el Estudio del H√≠gado, is an organization dedicated to advancing the understanding and treatment of liver diseases. AASLD provides evidence-based recommendations for the diagnosis and treatment of various liver conditions, including Wilson's disease. Through its guidelines and research initiatives, AASLD plays a crucial role in improving patient care and outcomes in the field of hepatology.EASL:La Asociaci√≥n Europea para el Estudio del H√≠gado (EASL) es una organizaci√≥n que tambi√©n ofrece directrices y recomendaciones para el manejo de enfermedades hep√°ticas, incluyendo la EW.SICILIA:Sicilia es una isla italiana donde la Enfermedad de Wilson se presenta con mayor frecuencia en comparaci√≥n con otras regiones.ITALIA:Italia, known as Italy, is a country where the Enfermedad de Wilson is notably more prevalent, particularly in its southern regions. Recent studies conducted in Italia have focused on the survival rates of patients suffering from s√≠ndrome de intestino corto. Additionally, Italia has established regulations for NED funding since 1988, reflecting its commitment to healthcare and research. However, it is also one of the eight countries that face challenges in data collection for epidemiological studies, which may impact the comprehensiveness of health-related research within the nation.S√çNTOMAS NEUROL√ìGICOS:Los s√≠ntomas neurol√≥gicos son manifestaciones cl√≠nicas que pueden aparecer en pacientes con Enfermedad de Wilson, relacionados con la acumulaci√≥n de cobre en el sistema nervioso.ANILLO DE KAYSER-FLEISCHER:El anillo de Kayser-Fleischer es un hallazgo cl√≠nico que se debe descartar en pacientes con sospecha de Enfermedad de Wilson, aunque su ausencia no excluye el diagn√≥stico.DOLOR DE CABEZA:El dolor de cabeza es uno de los s√≠ntomas que pueden presentarse en pacientes con Enfermedad de Wilson, especialmente en etapas avanzadas.TEMBLORES:Los temblores son un s√≠ntoma neurol√≥gico que puede manifestarse en pacientes con Enfermedad de Wilson debido a la acumulaci√≥n de cobre.DIFICULTAD DE COORDINACI√ìN:La dificultad de coordinaci√≥n es un s√≠ntoma neurol√≥gico que puede presentarse en pacientes con Enfermedad de Wilson.ABORTOS ESPONT√ÅNEOS:Los abortos espont√°neos repetidos e inexplicables son s√≠ntomas que pueden presentarse en mujeres premenop√°usicas con Enfermedad de Wilson.FATIGA:La fatiga es uno de los primeros s√≠ntomas que pueden aparecer en pacientes con Enfermedad de Wilson.)<|COMPLETE|>EW:EW refers to Wilson's disease, a genetic disorder characterized by excessive copper accumulation in the body, which primarily affects the liver and brain. This condition necessitates treatment to manage the copper levels and may ultimately require liver transplantation in severe cases.D-PENICILAMINA:D-Penicillamine is a chelating agent and a pharmaceutical treatment primarily used in the management of Wilson's disease, a genetic disorder that leads to excessive copper accumulation in the body. It functions by helping to eliminate excess copper, thereby reducing its levels in the body. D-Penicillamine is considered a first-line medication for this condition, effectively aiding in the treatment and management of copper toxicity associated with Wilson's disease.TRIENTINA:Trientina, also known as Trientine, is a medication utilized as an alternative treatment for Wilson's disease, a genetic disorder that leads to excessive accumulation of copper in the body. It functions as a chelator, effectively binding to excess copper and facilitating its removal from the body. Trientine is often noted for being better tolerated than D-Penicillamine, another chelating agent used in the treatment of Wilson's disease. Overall, Trientina serves as a valuable option for managing this condition by helping to reduce copper levels and mitigate its associated health risks.HEPATITIS AUTOINMUNE:Autoimmune hepatitis is a condition that can be confused with Wilson's disease, requiring careful diagnosis.FAMILIARES DE PRIMER GRADO:First-degree relatives are family members of a patient diagnosed with Wilson's disease (EW) who should be evaluated for the condition due to genetic factors.INSUFICIENCIA HEP√ÅTICA AGUDA:"INSUFICIENCIA HEP√ÅTICA AGUDA," or acute liver failure, is a critical and severe condition that can arise in patients suffering from Wilson's disease. This medical emergency necessitates immediate intervention to address the potentially life-threatening complications associated with the condition.CUPRURIA:Cupruria refers to the presence of excess copper in urine, which is a diagnostic indicator for Wilson's disease (EW).KAYSER-FLEISCHER RINGS:Kayser-Fleischer rings are deposits of copper in the cornea, often seen in patients with Wilson's disease, detectable by slit lamp examination.ACANTOSIS NIGRICANS:Acanthosis nigricans is a skin condition that may be associated with Wilson's disease, characterized by dark, velvety patches.CIRROSIS DESCOMPENSADA:Decompensated cirrhosis is a severe liver condition that can develop in patients with Wilson's disease, leading to complications and potential transplantation.KAYSER-FLEISCHER RING:Anillo de Kayser-Fleischer es un dep√≥sito de cobre que se puede observar en los ojos de los pacientes con enfermedad de Wilson, utilizado como un indicador diagn√≥stico.POLIARTRITIS:La poliartritis es una inflamaci√≥n de m√∫ltiples articulaciones que puede ser un s√≠ntoma asociado a la enfermedad de Wilson.FRACTURAS ESPONT√ÅNEAS:Las fracturas espont√°neas pueden ocurrir en pacientes con enfermedad de Wilson debido a la debilidad √≥sea.CALCIFICACIONES ARTICULARES:Las calcificaciones articulares son dep√≥sitos de calcio en las articulaciones que pueden estar presentes en pacientes con enfermedad de Wilson.CUCURRIA:La cupruria es la eliminaci√≥n de cobre en la orina, que se utiliza para evaluar la presencia de la enfermedad de Wilson.CUPREMIA:LEIPZIG:Leipzig is a city in Germany that hosted an international meeting regarding medical diagnostics and treatment methods.KING'S SCORE:King's score is a diagnostic tool developed to assess liver function based on specific biochemical parameters.PENICILAMINA:Penicillamine is a medication primarily used to treat Wilson's disease, a condition characterized by the accumulation of copper in the body. It works by reducing this copper buildup, thereby alleviating the symptoms associated with the disease. Additionally, penicillamine is known to cause abnormal taste sensations as a side effect.NAZER ET AL.:Nazer et al. are researchers who developed the King's score for assessing liver function.DHAWAN ET AL.:Dhawan et al. are researchers who modified the King's score for liver function assessment.CIRROSIS POSTNECR√ìTICA:Cirrhosis postnecr√≥tica is a chronic liver condition resulting from severe liver damage, often leading to liver failure, and is relevant in the context of liver disease diagnostics.FALLO HEP√ÅTICO FULMINANTE:Fallo hep√°tico fulminante is an acute liver failure condition that can occur suddenly and is critical in medical assessments.ANEMIA HEMOL√çTICA COOMBS NEGATIVO:Anemia hemol√≠tica Coombs negativo is a type of anemia that can be associated with liver disease and is part of the diagnostic criteria.CUERPOS DE RODANINA:Cuerpos de rhodanine are granules that can be detected in liver tissue and are relevant in the diagnosis of copper-related liver diseases.CRIBADO FAMILIAR:Cribado familiar refers to the genetic screening of family members for hereditary conditions, particularly relevant in the context of Wilson's disease.HEPATITIS CR√ìNICA ACTIVA:SYPRINE:Nombre comercial de trientina, un medicamento utilizado como quelante alternativo en pacientes intolerantes a la penicilaminaLABORATORIOS RUBIO:Compa√±√≠a farmac√©utica que comercializa D-penicilamina en Espa√±aLABORATORIOS MERCK:Compa√±√≠a farmac√©utica que comercializa Syprine, un medicamento para el tratamiento de la enfermedad de WilsonCUPRIP√âN:Cuprip√©n is the commercial name for D-penicilamina in Spain, used in the treatment of Wilson's diseaseASOCIACI√ìN ESPA√ëOLA DE FAMILIARES Y ENFERMOS DE WILSON:The Asociaci√≥n Espa√±ola de Familiares y Enfermos de Wilson is an organization that provides support for families and patients affected by Wilson's disease, offering resources and information through their website.ACETATO DE ZINC:Acetato de zinc is a treatment for Wilson's disease that blocks intestinal copper absorption and is used as a chronic maintenance therapy.S√çNDROME DE HEMOL√çTICO:Hemolytic syndrome is a condition that can occur as a complication of Wilson's disease due to liver dysfunction and copper toxicity.METALOTIONE√çNAS:Metallothioneins are proteins that bind metals, including copper, and play a role in the body's response to copper overload in Wilson's disease.QUINOLONAS:Quinolonas son una clase de antibi√≥ticos que pueden estar relacionados con la aparici√≥n de diarrea. Adem√°s, estos antibi√≥ticos pueden interactuar con minerales presentes en la dieta, lo que puede afectar su eficacia. Tambi√©n se ha observado que las quinolonas pueden interferir con tratamientos para la enfermedad de Wilson, especialmente en lo que respecta a la absorci√≥n de cobre.TETRACICLINAS:TETRACICLINAS, or tetracyclines, are a group of antibiotics known for their ability to form insoluble complexes with dietary minerals. This characteristic can affect the absorption and efficacy of the antibiotics when taken alongside certain foods or supplements. Additionally, tetracyclines can interact with treatments for Wilson's disease, which is a condition that affects copper metabolism in the body. This interaction may have implications for the management of patients undergoing treatment for this disease, highlighting the importance of considering potential drug interactions when prescribing tetracyclines.WILSON'S DISEASE:Wilson's disease is a genetic disorder characterized by the excessive accumulation of copper in the body. This condition can lead to a range of health issues, primarily affecting the liver and causing neurological problems. The disorder results from a defect in the body's ability to eliminate copper, leading to its toxic buildup and subsequent health complications.KALER SG:Kaler SG is a researcher and author who has contributed significantly to the understanding of ATP7A-related copper transport disorders, including Wilson's disease.NUTRITION AND DIETETICS:Nutrition and Dietetics is a field that studies dietary practices and their impact on health, including the management of conditions like Wilson's disease.NUTRITIONAL REVIEW:Nutritional Review is a publication that discusses various aspects of nutrition, including the regulation of copper absorption in the body.NATURAL REVIEWS NEUROLOGY:Natural Reviews Neurology is a journal that covers neurological disorders, including those related to copper metabolism such as Wilson's disease.WORLD JOURNAL OF HEPATOLOGY:The World Journal of Hepatology publishes reviews and research on liver diseases, including Wilson's disease.MENEKES DISEASE:Menkes disease is a genetic disorder that affects copper levels in the body, leading to copper deficiency and neurological problems, and is often studied in relation to Wilson's disease.DRUGS:Drugs is a journal that publishes research on pharmacology and treatment options for various diseases, including Wilson's disease.LIVER INTERNATIONAL:Liver International is a journal that focuses on liver diseases and their management, including conditions like Wilson's disease.CLIN GASTROENTEROL HEPATOL:Clin Gastroenterol Hepatol is a medical journal that publishes research related to gastroenterology and hepatology.J HEPATOL:J Hepatol is a medical journal focusing on liver diseases and hepatology research.IR MED J:Ir Med J is the Irish Medical Journal that publishes articles on various medical topics, including clinical studies.EUROPEAN JOURNAL OF MEDICAL GENETICS:The European Journal of Medical Genetics publishes research on genetic disorders, including Wilson's disease.ANA BEL√âN MA√ëAS MART√çNEZ:Ana Bel√©n Ma√±as Mart√≠nez is a researcher contributing to studies on nephrolithiasis and dietary interventions.ANA AGUDO TABUENCA:Ana Agudo Tabuenca is a researcher focused on diet therapy and nutrition in clinical settings.DIETA CONTROLADA EN PURINAS:Dieta controlada en purinas refers to a dietary regimen aimed at managing uric acid levels, particularly in patients with conditions like nephrolithiasis.NATIONAL HEALTH AND NUTRITION EXAMINATION SURVEY:NHANES es una encuesta que proporciona datos sobre la prevalencia de litiasis renal y otras condiciones de salud en la poblaci√≥n de EE.UU.C√ÅLCULOS RENALES:C√°lculos renales are solid masses made of crystals that originate in the kidneys. These formations can lead to severe pain and various health complications. They are a significant concern in renal health, as their presence can disrupt normal kidney function and result in discomfort for the affected individual.C√ÅLCULO:C√°lculo es una formaci√≥n s√≥lida en el ri√±√≥n que puede causar c√≥lico nefr√≠tico y otros problemas de salud.C√ìLICO NEFR√çTICO:C√≥lico nefr√≠tico es la forma de presentaci√≥n cl√≠nica m√°s com√∫n de la litiasis renal, que ocurre cuando un c√°lculo se desprende o se rompe.MODIFICACIONES DIET√âTICAS:Las modificaciones diet√©ticas son parte del manejo de la litiasis renal, orientadas a reducir la formaci√≥n de c√°lculos.XANTINA OXIDASA:La xantina oxidasa es una enzima que cataliza la conversi√≥n de hipoxantina y guanina a xantina y √°cido √∫rico.ALANTO√çNA:La alanto√≠na es el producto final de la transformaci√≥n del √°cido √∫rico en mam√≠feros.PURINAS:PURINAS are molecules that are integral components of nucleic acids and are metabolized in the body to produce uric acid. These compounds, found in various foods, can lead to increased uric acid production, which may contribute to conditions such as hyperuricemia. The metabolism of purines is significant as it can elevate uric acid levels, potentially impacting health and leading to related medical conditions.FEBUXOSTAT:El febuxostat es un inhibidor utilizado en el tratamiento de la hiperuricemia, que afecta la producci√≥n de √°cido √∫rico.HIPOXANTINA:Hipoxantina is an enzyme that plays a crucial role in the degradation of purines within the human body. It facilitates the conversion of guanosine into guanine, thereby participating in the metabolic processes associated with purines. Additionally, hipoxantina serves as an intermediary in purine metabolism, where it is further converted into xantina. This highlights its importance in the biochemical pathways that manage purine levels in the body.GUANINA:Guanina, also known as guanine, is a purine base that plays a crucial role in the structure of nucleic acids in living organisms. It is produced from guanosine and is involved in various metabolic processes. In addition, guanina is metabolized through intermediates such as hypoxanthine and xanthine, ultimately leading to the formation of uric acid. This highlights its significance not only in genetic material but also in metabolic pathways within the body.XANTINA:Xantina is a compound that is formed during the metabolism of purines. It specifically arises from the degradation of purines, particularly through the breakdown of hypoxanthine and guanine. Ultimately, xantina is converted into uric acid, which is a key component in the purine metabolism pathway.C√ÅLCULOS DE √ÅCIDO √öRICO:Los c√°lculos de √°cido √∫rico son dep√≥sitos s√≥lidos que se forman en la orina debido a la baja solubilidad del √°cido √∫rico.GUANOSINA:La guanosina es un nucle√≥sido que se forma a partir de la degradaci√≥n de las purinas y se convierte en guanina.ADENOSINA:La adenosina es un nucle√≥sido que se forma a partir de la degradaci√≥n de las purinas y se convierte en inosina.NUCLE√ìSIDO:Los nucle√≥sidos son compuestos formados por una base nitrogenada unida a un az√∫car, que son productos intermedios en el metabolismo de las purinas.URATO:URICASA:Uricasa is an enzyme that converts uric acid into allantoin, which is more easily excreted from the body.GOTA:GOTA, also known as gout, is a form of inflammatory arthritis that arises from the body's inflammatory response to the deposition of monosodium urate crystals in joints and tissues, primarily due to elevated levels of uric acid. This condition is characterized by sudden and severe attacks of pain, swelling, and redness in the affected joints. GOTA is often associated with the formation of uric acid stones, which can occur as a result of the high uric acid levels in the body. Overall, GOTA represents a clinical syndrome that significantly impacts the quality of life of those affected, manifesting through intense joint pain and inflammation.TRASTORNOS MIELOPROLIFERATIVOS:Trastornos mieloproliferativos are disorders of the bone marrow that can result in increased production of √°cido √∫rico due to high cell turnover.TRASTORNOS LINFOPROLIFERATIVOS:Trastornos linfoproliferativos are conditions that involve the proliferation of lymphocytes, which can also lead to elevated levels of √°cido √∫rico.SORIASIS:Sor√≠asis is a skin condition that can contribute to increased production of √°cido √∫rico in the body.DEFICIT DE VITAMINA B12:Deficit de vitamina B12 can lead to metabolic changes that may increase the production of √°cido √∫rico.DIUR√âTICOS TIAZ√çDICOS:Diur√©ticos tiaz√≠dicos are common medications that can decrease the renal elimination of √°cido √∫rico, contributing to hyperuricemia.SALICILATOS:Salicilatos at low doses can also reduce the elimination of √°cido √∫rico, leading to increased levels in the blood.ALANTOINA:C√ÅLCULO RENAL:C√°lculo renal refers to kidney stones, which are hard deposits made of minerals and salts that form inside the kidneys. These deposits can lead to significant pain and complications within the urinary tract. In summary, c√°lculos renales are characterized as hard mineral and salt formations in the kidneys, often resulting in discomfort and potential health issues.HIPERCALCIURIA:HIPERCALCIURIA is a condition characterized by an excessive amount of calcium in the urine. This elevated calcium level can predispose individuals to the formation of kidney stones and is often associated with various metabolic disorders. The condition is recognized in both English and Spanish, with the Spanish description emphasizing the same key points regarding the elevated calcium levels and the potential risk of developing renal calculi.ENFERMEDAD VASCULAR MANIFIESTA:La enfermedad vascular manifiesta se refiere a condiciones en las que hay evidencia de enfermedad en los vasos sangu√≠neos, lo que puede estar relacionado con la hiperuricemia y el riesgo cardiovascular.HIPERPARATIROIDISMO PRIMARIO:Hiperparatiroidismo primario is a medical condition where the parathyroid glands produce too much parathyroid hormone, leading to elevated calcium levels in the blood.HIPOCAUCALCEMIA IDIOP√ÅTICA:Hipocalcemia idiop√°tica is a condition where there is a decrease in calcium levels in the urine without a known cause, often associated with significant bone mass loss.OXALATO C√ÅLCICO MONOHIDRATO:Oxalato c√°lcico monohidratado, also known as whewellita, is a type of kidney stone that forms in alkaline urine and is associated with specific urinary pH levels.OXALATO C√ÅLCICO DIHIDRATO:Oxalato c√°lcico dihidratado, or weddellite, is another type of calcium oxalate stone that can form in the kidneys, particularly in acidic urine.FOSFATO C√ÅLCICO:Fosfato c√°lcico, or brushite, is a type of kidney stone that forms in specific urinary pH conditions and is associated with hypercalciuria.FOSFATO AM√ìNICO MAGN√âSICO:Fosfato am√≥nico magn√©sico, or struvite, is a type of kidney stone that often forms in the presence of urinary infections and alkaline urine.CISTINA:Cistina is an amino acid that can lead to the formation of kidney stones in individuals with a genetic disorder known as cystinuria.OXALOBACTER FORMIGENES:Oxalobacter formigenes is a gram-negative anaerobic bacterium that plays a role in degrading oxalate in the intestine, potentially affecting oxalate absorption and kidney stone formation.HIPOXALURIA:HIPOXALURIA refers to a condition characterized by low levels of oxalate in the urine. This condition can influence kidney stone formation, as oxalate is a key component in the development of certain types of stones. Additionally, dietary factors can play a significant role in the levels of oxalate present in the urine, further impacting the condition.HIPOXALURIA IDIOP√ÅTICA:Hipoxaluria idiop√°tica is a rare condition characterized by low oxalate levels in the urine without a known cause.HIPOXALURIA SECUNDARIA:Hipoxaluria secundaria is a condition where low oxalate levels in the urine are caused by an underlying medical condition or dietary factors.HIPOXALURIA PRIMARIA:HIPOXALURIA PRIMARIA is a genetic disorder characterized by abnormal oxalate levels in the body. There are two distinct forms of this condition: one leads to hyperoxaluria, where the body produces excessive amounts of oxalate, resulting in the formation of kidney stones. Conversely, the other form is associated with hypoxaluria, which is marked by low levels of oxalate in the urine and may potentially impact kidney function. This duality in the condition highlights the complexity of HIPOXALURIA PRIMARIA and its varying effects on renal health.HIPOXALURIA RELATIVA:Hipoxaluria relativa refers to a condition where oxalate levels are low relative to other substances in the urine, which may influence kidney stone risk.HIPOXALURIA CR√ìNICA:Hipoxaluria cr√≥nica is a long-term condition characterized by persistently low oxalate levels in the urine, which can have various health implications.HIPOXALURIA AGUDA:Hipoxaluria aguda is a sudden onset of low oxalate levels in the urine, which may be triggered by dietary changes or medical conditions.HIPOXALURIA FAMILIAR:Hipoxaluria familiar is a hereditary condition that leads to low oxalate levels in the urine, affecting family members across generations.HIPOCAUCALCEMIA:LITIASIS:LITIASIS refers to the formation of stones in the urinary tract, often associated with various metabolic disorders and dietary factors.CISTINURIA:CISTINURIA is a hereditary tubulopathy affecting the transport of cystine and basic amino acids, leading to the formation of kidney stones.ACIDO URICO:ACIDO URICO refers to a metabolic condition characterized by the formation of uric acid stones in the urinary tract. Uric acid is a waste product that results from the breakdown of purines, which are substances found in certain foods and drinks. Elevated levels of uric acid in the body can lead to various health issues, including hyperuricemia, a condition marked by excessive uric acid in the blood, and gout, a type of inflammatory arthritis caused by the accumulation of uric acid crystals in the joints.CITRATO:CITRATO is a significant inhibitor of urinary crystallization, especially in the formation of calcium oxalate crystals. This compound plays a crucial role in preventing the formation of calcium stones in the urine, and its effectiveness is influenced by dietary intake.HIPOCASTRITURIA:HIPOCASTRITURIA refers to a condition characterized by low levels of citrate in urine, which can lead to increased risk of stone formation.HIPOCRITURIA:HIPOCRITURIA is a condition where there is a deficiency of citrate in urine, leading to a higher risk of kidney stones.ACIDOSIS TUBULAR RENAL DISTAL:ACIDOSIS TUBULAR RENAL DISTAL is a renal condition characterized by its impact on urinary pH and the potential for stone formation. This condition affects the kidneys' ability to properly acidify urine, leading to various metabolic disturbances.HYPERCITRATURIA:HYPERCITRATURIA is a condition characterized by high levels of citrate in urine, which can help prevent stone formation.HYPERURICOSURIA:HYPERURICOSURIA is a condition involving excessive uric acid in the urine, which can lead to the formation of uric acid stones.PROBENECID:Probenecid is a medication that plays a significant role in increasing uric acid excretion in urine, which can potentially influence the formation of kidney stones. It is primarily utilized in the treatment of gout and is effective in managing conditions associated with elevated uric acid levels. By enhancing the excretion of uric acid, Probenecid helps to alleviate symptoms related to gout and reduces the risk of kidney stone formation.SULFINPIRAZONA:SULFINPIRAZONA is a drug that can help lower uric acid levels in the body, influencing the risk of uric acid stone formation.BENZBROMARONA:BENZBROMARONA is a medication used to treat high uric acid levels, which can impact the formation of uric acid stones.LOSARTAN:LOSARTAN is an antihypertensive medication that may influence uric acid levels and thus affect stone formation.EVALUACI√ìN METAB√ìLICA:Evaluaci√≥n metab√≥lica is a diagnostic process to assess the metabolic factors contributing to kidney stone formation.HISTORIA LITI√ÅSICA:Historia liti√°sica refers to the patient's history of kidney stones, including the number and type of stones previously formed.DIETAS MALABSORTIVAS:Dietas malabsortivas are diets that lead to poor absorption of nutrients, which can contribute to the formation of kidney stones.ACETAZOLAMINA:Acetazolamina is a medication used to treat certain types of kidney stones and other conditions related to fluid retention.TRIAMTERENE:Triamterene is a diuretic medication that can be used in the management of kidney stones.INDINAVIR:Indinavir is an antiviral medication that can have side effects related to kidney function.ANATOMICAL ALTERATIONS:Anatomical alterations in the kidneys can lead to conditions such as kidney stones and other urinary tract issues.BLOOD AND URINE ANALYSIS:Blood and urine analysis are essential diagnostic tools for evaluating kidney function and identifying potential causes of nephrolithiasis.IMAGING STUDIES:Imaging studies help identify anatomical abnormalities and assess metabolic activity related to kidney stones.24-HOUR URINE COLLECTION:24-hour urine collection is a method used to evaluate the composition of urine and assess risk factors for kidney stone formation.CITRUS JUICES:Citrus juices, particularly orange juice, are recognized for their potential protective effects against kidney stones, primarily attributed to their high citrate content. These juices are known to increase urinary citrate levels, which is beneficial in reducing the risk of stone formation. The high potassium citrate content in citrus juices further contributes to this protective effect, making them a valuable addition to the diet for those concerned about kidney stone development.SUGARY SODAS:Sugary sodas may increase the risk of kidney stones and are generally advised against in patients with a history of nephrolithiasis.SPORTS DRINKS:Sports drinks containing electrolytes and carbohydrates do not significantly affect urinary excretion of calcium or other salts.KIDNEY STONES:Kidney stones are solid masses made of crystals that originate in the kidneys. They can cause significant pain and complications for those affected.URIC ACID:Uric acid is a waste product formed from the breakdown of purines, and its levels are significant in the formation of certain types of kidney stones.OXALATE:Oxalate is a compound commonly found in a variety of foods. It has the potential to combine with calcium, which can lead to the formation of kidney stones. Additionally, the levels of oxalate in urine can be influenced by dietary choices, further contributing to the risk of kidney stone development.PHOSPHATE:Phosphate is a chemical compound that can also contribute to the formation of kidney stones.RENAL ANATOMY:SOCIETY OF UROLOGY:The Society of Urology provides guidelines for the prevention of kidney stones, including recommendations on calcium intake.OXALATO:Oxalato is a compound commonly found in various foods. It is known to contribute to the formation of kidney stones, particularly when consumed in high amounts or when it is not adequately bound by calcium. This dual role highlights the importance of managing oxalate intake to mitigate the risk of kidney stone formation.PROTE√çNAS ANIMALES:Animal proteins should be limited to 0.8 g/day to help prevent kidney stones, except for dairy products.ACELGA:Swiss chard is a leafy green vegetable that is rich in oxalate, which can contribute to kidney stone development.CACAO:CACAO is a sweetened cocoa product that is derived from cacao beans. It is important to note that CACAO should not be given to infants before the age of one due to its sugar content. Additionally, cocoa, which is produced from cacao beans, contains oxalate, a compound that may increase the risk of kidney stones. Therefore, while CACAO can be enjoyed by older children and adults, caution is advised regarding its consumption by infants and individuals susceptible to kidney stones.FRAMBUESAS:Raspberries are fruits that contain oxalate, which can be a contributing factor to kidney stone formation.FRESAS:Fresas, commonly known as strawberries, are a type of fruit that is high in fiber. They are recognized for their nutritional benefits, including their fiber content. However, it is important to note that strawberries also contain oxalates, which may increase the risk of kidney stones in susceptible individuals.BEBIDAS A BASE DE COLA:Cola beverages can contain oxalate, which may contribute to the risk of developing kidney stones.ASOCIACI√ìN EUROPEA DE UROLOG√çA:The European Association of Urology provides guidelines for the treatment of urolithiasis and dietary recommendations for patients with hyperuricemia.HIDRATOS DE CARBONO SIMPLES:Simple carbohydrates are dietary components that can increase calcium absorption in the intestines and promote renal excretion of calcium and oxalate.ALIMENTOS RICOS EN PURINAS:Foods high in purines include animal meats, fish, and certain vegetables, which can elevate uric acid levels and contribute to hyperuricemia.(N␀␀␀␀␀␀çN␀␀␀␀␀␀˜N␀␀␀␀␀␀O␀␀␀␀␀␀îO␀␀␀␀␀␀P␀␀␀␀␀␀yP␀␀␀␀␀␀›P␀␀␀␀␀␀_Q␀␀␀␀␀␀ﬂQ␀␀␀␀␀␀2S␀␀␀␀␀␀∑S␀␀␀␀␀␀LT␀␀␀␀␀␀ V␀␀␀␀␀␀~X␀␀␀␀␀␀ËX␀␀␀␀␀␀7Y␀␀␀␀␀␀≤Y␀␀␀␀␀␀XZ␀␀␀␀␀␀˜Z␀␀␀␀␀␀∫[␀␀␀␀␀␀0\␀␀␀␀␀␀¯\␀␀␀␀␀␀å]␀␀␀␀␀␀_␀␀␀␀␀␀à_␀␀␀␀␀␀˘_␀␀␀␀␀␀z`␀␀␀␀␀␀	a␀␀␀␀␀␀Åa␀␀␀␀␀␀˚a␀␀␀␀␀␀nb␀␀␀␀␀␀Œb␀␀␀␀␀␀Bc␀␀␀␀␀␀ßc␀␀␀␀␀␀d␀␀␀␀␀␀ïd␀␀␀␀␀␀˘d␀␀␀␀␀␀ve␀␀␀␀␀␀‘e␀␀␀␀␀␀Rf␀␀␀␀␀␀‹f␀␀␀␀␀␀Áh␀␀␀␀␀␀åi␀␀␀␀␀␀j␀␀␀␀␀␀kj␀␀␀␀␀␀ák␀␀␀␀␀␀l␀␀␀␀␀␀¥l␀␀␀␀␀␀m␀␀␀␀␀␀om␀␀␀␀␀␀On␀␀␀␀␀␀ôo␀␀␀␀␀␀Úo␀␀␀␀␀␀Ep␀␀␀␀␀␀√p␀␀␀␀␀␀Sq␀␀␀␀␀␀␀r␀␀␀␀␀␀r␀␀␀␀␀␀s␀␀␀␀␀␀§s␀␀␀␀␀␀,t␀␀␀␀␀␀§u␀␀␀␀␀␀v␀␀␀␀␀␀îv␀␀␀␀␀␀w␀␀␀␀␀␀éw␀␀␀␀␀␀	x␀␀␀␀␀␀êx␀␀␀␀␀␀y␀␀␀␀␀␀ày␀␀␀␀␀␀z␀␀␀␀␀␀òz␀␀␀␀␀␀{␀␀␀␀␀␀˙|␀␀␀␀␀␀t}␀␀␀␀␀␀ÿ}␀␀␀␀␀␀<~␀␀␀␀␀␀†~␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀f␀␀␀␀␀␀¿␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä␀␀␀␀␀␀ÅÄ␀␀␀␀␀␀„Ä␀␀␀␀␀␀WÅ␀␀␀␀␀␀¡Å␀␀␀␀␀␀)Ç␀␀␀␀␀␀ëÇ␀␀␀␀␀␀ÛÇ␀␀␀␀␀␀|Ñ␀␀␀␀␀␀ÁÑ␀␀␀␀␀␀NÖ␀␀␀␀␀␀¢Ö␀␀␀␀␀␀ÓÖ␀␀␀␀␀␀8Ü␀␀␀␀␀␀éÜ␀␀␀␀␀␀⁄Ü␀␀␀␀␀␀&á␀␀␀␀␀␀tá␀␀␀␀␀␀ƒá␀␀␀␀␀␀à␀␀␀␀␀␀Çà␀␀␀␀␀␀â␀␀␀␀␀␀úâ␀␀␀␀␀␀ä␀␀␀␀␀␀´ä␀␀␀␀␀␀ã␀␀␀␀␀␀åã␀␀␀␀␀␀å␀␀␀␀␀␀‹å␀␀␀␀␀␀„ê␀␀␀␀␀␀bë␀␀␀␀␀␀˙ë␀␀␀␀␀␀äí␀␀␀␀␀␀ì␀␀␀␀␀␀,ì␀␀␀␀␀␀î␀␀␀␀␀␀Uï␀␀␀␀␀␀ˆï␀␀␀␀␀␀}ñ␀␀␀␀␀␀$ó␀␀␀␀␀␀Æó␀␀␀␀␀␀1ò␀␀␀␀␀␀:ô␀␀␀␀␀␀Ëô␀␀␀␀␀␀€ö␀␀␀␀␀␀yõ␀␀␀␀␀␀˚õ␀␀␀␀␀␀aú␀␀␀␀␀␀Óú␀␀␀␀␀␀¨†␀␀␀␀␀␀[°␀␀␀␀␀␀¢␀␀␀␀␀␀ñ£␀␀␀␀␀␀§␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä§␀␀␀␀␀␀•␀␀␀␀␀␀´•␀␀␀␀␀␀/¶␀␀␀␀␀␀¡¶␀␀␀␀␀␀Nß␀␀␀␀␀␀¡ß␀␀␀␀␀␀◊ß␀␀␀␀␀␀°®␀␀␀␀␀␀;©␀␀␀␀␀␀Ì©␀␀␀␀␀␀D´␀␀␀␀␀␀Á´␀␀␀␀␀␀Q≠␀␀␀␀␀␀ﬂÆ␀␀␀␀␀␀9∞␀␀␀␀␀␀»∞␀␀␀␀␀␀Y±␀␀␀␀␀␀q≤␀␀␀␀␀␀˙≤␀␀␀␀␀␀d≥␀␀␀␀␀␀¿≥␀␀␀␀␀␀bµ␀␀␀␀␀␀F∑␀␀␀␀␀␀©∑␀␀␀␀␀␀&∏␀␀␀␀␀␀ƒ∏␀␀␀␀␀␀8π␀␀␀␀␀␀¨π␀␀␀␀␀␀∫␀␀␀␀␀␀v∫␀␀␀␀␀␀ª␀␀␀␀␀␀ìª␀␀␀␀␀␀º␀␀␀␀␀␀rº